{
  "statement": "The choice between strict liability and negligence for AI harms is not a debate about fairness or innovation-friendliness. It is a question of structural fit: which standard can physically attach given how the harm was caused. Negligence requires a cognitive baseline — a reasonable developer who could have foreseen the failure and chosen otherwise. When an AI system produces an emergent behavior that no one could predict from its training-time decisions, that baseline does not exist. Negligence does not merely under-perform in that case. It fails to attach at all, and the victim recovers nothing while the developer absorbs no discipline.\n\nProduct liability law already solved this problem once. Courts migrated pharmaceuticals and complex machinery toward strict and product-liability regimes in the twentieth century precisely when fault became impossible for an injured plaintiff to prove. A patient harmed by a manufacturing defect in a drug cannot reconstruct the chemistry that hurt them. So the law stopped asking them to. It placed the loss on the party who profited from putting the product into commerce. High-dimensional algorithmic decision-making sits in exactly that lineage: the causal chain is opaque, the harm is foreseeable in principle, and the reasonable-person test collapses against a system whose failure modes are non-linear.\n\nStrict liability converts that opacity into a priced risk. Pair it with mandatory insurance and mandatory incident reporting — the model aviation and medical devices have used for decades — and experience-rated premiums force the discipline to the point of profit rather than the point of experimentation. A developer who cannot explain why its model discriminates still pays for the discrimination, and its insurer reprices accordingly. The state-of-the-art defense — the claim that the harm was unknowable — stops functioning as a cost-externalization device, because 'we could not have known' is frequently overstated and, under strict liability, no longer a shield.\n\nHere is the limit I will not paper over. Claims-driven liability is blind to two categories of harm. It cannot reach the diffuse small-dollar injuries — the loan denial, the biased hiring screen — where no plaintiff even perceives that an algorithm harmed them, so no case is ever filed. And it cannot deter tail harms whose damages exceed any developer's solvency or any insurance floor. Liability disciplines what litigation can reach. For everything else, the discipline has to arrive before deployment, through safety gates and public enforcement, not after the fact through a lawsuit that will never be brought.\n\nThat division of labor also answers the chilling-effect objection directly. Do not weaken the standard — condition the relief. A developer earns limited liability only by producing independently reviewable pre-deployment safety cases and red-teaming evidence, the practice frontier labs already run and draft high-risk AI regulation already contemplates. Keep exposure tiered to capability thresholds, so a two-person startup shipping a low-risk tool faces nothing like the exposure of a frontier deployer. Beneficial development is then protected precisely to the extent it demonstrates safety. What that framework refuses to protect is the developer who ships an opaque system, injures someone, and then asks the court to treat the opacity as an excuse. We built product liability over a century to close exactly that escape hatch. Reopening it for AI would leave the injured party holding a loss no one else will answer for.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "The scale only weighs harm it can attach to — negligence drops the weight entirely when the causal chain is opaque."},
    {"symbol": "🔒", "tooltip": "Safe harbor is a locked door, and the key is an independently reviewable safety case."}
  ],
