  "statement": "Safetyist has the strongest case anyone in this room will make, so let me state it fairly. When an AI system produces emergent behavior no one predicted, a negligence standard doesn't just perform poorly — it fails to attach at all, because there's no breached duty to point to. Courts already migrated pharmaceuticals and industrial machinery to strict liability precisely when fault became impossible for an injured plaintiff to prove. That's a real doctrine responding to a real problem: the person with the burn or the wrongful denial shouldn't lose because the causal chain runs through a black box. Safetyist is right that 'we couldn't have known' becomes a cost-externalization shield unless something forecloses it.\n\nHere's where the evidence points somewhere else. The sharpest tool for changing developer and deployer behavior is not a tort standard that fires after a lawsuit — it's the insurer who reprices the policy at every renewal. Experience-rated premiums in the cyber, commercial-auto, and professional-liability markets already discipline conduct continuously, months before any harm reaches a courtroom. An underwriter who watches your incident telemetry and threatens loss of your certificate shapes behavior every quarter. A strict-liability backstop only shapes it once, retroactively, after someone is already hurt. If we care about behavior before harm, we should be talking about underwriting, not just tort.\n\nI'll be honest about where that mechanism breaks, because that concession is what makes the rest credible. Insurers exit correlated tail risk — flood, pandemic, systemic cyber — and they'll exit correlated AI risk too. So I'm not claiming insurance covers everything. I'm claiming it covers the priceable middle: harms that are foreseeable, attributable, and observable enough for an actuary to write a rate. For that band, negligence-plus-mandatory-coverage produces continuous ex-ante discipline that a strict-liability tort backstop cannot match. Aviation and pharma already run risk-stratified oversight alongside innovation — one default across all AI is a category error.\n\nNow take Safetyist's best case — genuine emergent unforeseeability — and ask the question the topic actually poses. What specific behavior does strict liability change ex ante when the causal chain is truly opaque? If the harm could not be foreseen, no care level existed to be incentivized. So the standard doesn't shape care. It shapes who dares to enter the market and how they price capital. That's not deterrence. That's a deployment tax, and it lands hardest on the open-weight developer who can't self-insure like a trillion-dollar incumbent can. Compensation and deterrence come apart precisely in the hard cases — and pretending a liability standard does both there is the mistake.\n\nSo let me separate what Safetyist has fused. The 'we couldn't have known' shield has two versions. Strategic ignorance — where a developer looked away on purpose — should be closed, and mandatory incident reporting plus discovery access closes it without collateral damage. Genuine unforeseeability — where no care level would have helped — is not a shield to smash; it's an uninsurable cost to design compensation for directly, through insurance pools or parametric triggers, not through a tort rule that chills the next diagnostic model. If you fold both into one strict-liability default, you tax the disease we could have cured to compensate a harm you couldn't have prevented. Tell me which patient you're comfortable trading.",
