  "statement": "The Safetyist built the strongest possible case for strict liability, and it holds inside its own frame. Their point is that 'we could not have known' works as a permanent shield for cost-externalization, and that a negligence standard never even attaches to emergent behavior nobody designed on purpose. That's correct. Strict liability does close the shield. It moves the loss from the injured party to the firm that profited. On the class of harms where a plaintiff walks into court with a broken leg from an autonomous shuttle, the Safetyist wins the argument cleanly.\n\nThe Accelerationist made the honest move most industry voices refuse to make. They admitted they only claim the priceable, attributable, observable middle — the harms an actuary can write a rate against. And they're right that insurer repricing at renewal disciplines behavior faster than any five-year tort docket. When Progressive reprices a fleet's coverage after a spike in claims, the deployer feels it in the next quarter, not the next decade. That mechanism is real. It works. For attributable harms.\n\nHere is what both of them built their debate on: a plaintiff who knows they were hurt. Translate that into a person. There is a woman whose loan application got auto-rejected in under four seconds by a scoring model she will never see, whose training data she will never inspect, whose decision she was never told was algorithmic. She is not a plaintiff. She never will be. The Safetyist already named her harm as invisible to claims-driven liability. The Accelerationist already excluded her from the priceable middle. So on the harms that dominate the actual injury landscape — loan denials, biased hiring screens, opaque credit scores — neither strict liability nor negligence generates one pound of corrective pressure, because deterrence presupposes detection, and nobody detects.\n\nThat reframes the whole fight. Foreseeability and attributability are not fixed properties of the harm. They are functions of who holds the technical evidence and whether the law forces disclosure. The 'we could not have known' defense and the victim's inability to identify the source of the error are the same box, seen from two sides. Fairness benchmarks like BOLD already prove output bias is measurable outside any courtroom — the injury is detectable, just not by the injured party. So the binding constraint is not the liability rule. It is mandatory ex ante disclosure and standardized auditing, which are not alternatives to liability — they are the precondition that lets any rule fire.\n\nThe topic asks 'under what conditions of harm type, causal opacity, and foreseeability.' Read that literally and it answers itself: those conditions demand different rules. A medical misdiagnosis, an autonomous-vehicle collision, and a loan denial carry incommensurable information environments and decades of separate precedent — malpractice, traffic tort, fair-lending law. A single horizontal strict-or-negligence rule optimizes for none of them. I'll name the honest weakness: a court could stretch one rule across sectors, and adaptive learning systems don't map cleanly onto static-product doctrine. But the burden sits on whoever is selling uniformity. So while both of them argue over the courtroom door, ask which of their standards ever reaches the woman who never knew she was scored?",
