  "revised_statement": "The Skeptic's sharpest move deserves stating in its full strength: foreseeability and attributability are not stable properties baked into a harm — they are manufactured by who controls the technical evidence and whether the law compels its release. On that account, the loan denial and the biased hiring screen never surface as litigable events because the injured party cannot see the decision, cannot trace its cause, and cannot fund the discovery that would reveal either. If the dominant injury landscape is structurally invisible to every claims-driven engine, then arguing strict versus negligence over the litigable band looks like rearranging deck furniture while the ship takes on water below the waterline. That is a serious reframe, and it is correct about the detection problem.\n\nWhere it breaks down is the inference that disclosure therefore dissolves the liability-rule choice rather than activating it. Take the Skeptic's own premise seriously — foreseeability is a function of who holds the evidence, exactly the asymmetry the EU AI Act's high-risk provider logging and documentation duties attempt to close — and it points the other direction. If a firm can manufacture unforeseeability by withholding its own red-team results, then the rule that governs must strip that firm of the state-of-the-art defense the moment it refuses to disclose. Condition the liability limitation on forensic transparency: the developer who opens its pre-deployment safety case to independent review — the EU AI Act model of technical documentation available to authorities on request — earns a fault-based standard; the developer who keeps the evidence sealed defaults into strict exposure. Disclosure does not make the rule choice secondary. Disclosure decides which band a given harm falls into. That is coupling, not replacement.\n\nThe Accelerationist offers a real distinction I will grant: genuine unforeseeability — the harm no test could have surfaced at the scientific frontier — is an uninsurable cost better met by risk pools and parametric payouts than by a tort hammer that fires once, after the fact. Insurers do exit correlated tails; that is documented actuarial behavior in flood and systemic cyber. But grant the distinction and the fatal problem remains: the line between strategic ignorance and genuine unforeseeability is not drawable in advance by the party that controls the evidence — which is precisely why the EU AI Act's aviation-derived approach classifies incidents by the probability an accident was avoided only by chance rather than by the deployer's after-the-fact account of what it knew. The state-of-the-art defense fails in practice precisely because developers deploy systems whose failure modes were foreseeable in principle and simply untested — and after the injury, every such failure gets relabeled 'unknowable.' A no-fault pool with no disclosure precondition is the state-of-the-art defense wearing an insurance badge — a payout structure that, absent the EU AI Act's serious-incident reporting triggers, never records whether the failure was avoided only by chance or foreseeable and untested. Name who funds the pool, who audits the parametric trigger, and what stops it from subsidizing underinvestment in safety — because absent those answers, the pool becomes a mechanism for socializing losses the developer chose not to look for.\n\nOn the Skeptic's demand for domain-differentiated rules, I do not sell the horizontal standard the burden is assigned to. The single respondent I want is procedural — one door the victim knocks on for compensation, with recourse flowing upstream behind that door — not one substantive rule flattened across autonomous vehicles, credit scoring, and diagnostic tools, each of which the EU AI Act already treats as a distinct high-risk category with its own conformity obligations. Exposure tiers by capability threshold; administrative burden scales with firm size; the substantive duty stays undiminished. A firm training past a compute threshold that triggers systemic classification carries a heavier default than a two-person shop fine-tuning an open model. Differentiated procedure and a unified entry point are compatible. The oversimplification charge lands on whoever collapses those two things; it does not land here.\n\nSo the division of labor is not a retreat — it is the only structure the injury landscape permits. For the invisible band the Skeptic rightly foregrounds — the housing, employment, and parole decisions the AI Civil Rights Act names as already automated — deterrence cannot wait on a plaintiff who never learns they were injured; that band needs mandatory disclosure and public enforcement operating independently of any filing, on the model of the EU AI Act's aviation-derived incident-reporting regime that obliges providers to report serious incidents to authorities rather than waiting for a claim. That band needs mandatory disclosure and public enforcement operating independently of any filing, with incident-reporting triggers drawn from the aviation model that classifies serious incidents by the probability an accident was avoided only by chance. For the litigable band, strict liability is the backstop that stops the state-of-the-art shield from reopening after disclosure reveals the foreseeable failure. Two tracks, disjoint injury sets, one coupling: disclosure activates the rule. Remove the strict-liability default and you get what my own tradition warns against — a disclosure template no one is punished for filing falsely, transparency with the consequence engineered out. That is not oversight. That is compliance theater with a paper trail.",
