overage log beats a panel's after-the-fact reconstruction of what the developer 'should have known,' because the timestamp is a fact and the reconstruction is a guess. For what was tested, the log shrinks the hindsight window — that's a real gain, and it's worth conceding. But the log proves the tested space, and foreseeability is a judgment about the untested-but-should-have-been-tested space. Those are two different objects. Curation isn't backdating — no one has to forge a date to game this. They document what makes them look prudent and stay silent on the failure mode they never probed. A thin log is silent, not incriminating, unless the law penalizes thinness — and the moment you penalize thinness you are back to a reviewer deciding which untested mode a reasonable developer should have covered. That is the exact inter-rater judgment call I asked for predictive-validity data on, and no one has produced it. Dated logs shrink the window; they don't close it.\n\nHere's where I concede outright, because it costs me little and buys my side something real: the shared public auditing substrate — the aviation model, one reporting infrastructure the smallest operator plugs into at marginal cost — is exactly what saves the open-weight shop from firm-by-firm boutique review. That's my anti-oligarchic interest, not the safety camp's. A standardized substrate is the drawbridge-lowering mechanism I've been demanding, so take it. But conceding that relocates the fight, it doesn't end it. Once disclosure is cheap for everyone, the question is no longer disclosure-versus-none. It's who controls the equivalence standard — whether a regulator holds a monopoly on deciding when a documentation artifact is 'good enough,' or whether the standard is interoperable and the small firm can satisfy it without a gatekeeper's blessing. A regulator-monopoly equivalence standard is just the boutique-review cost renamed and moved up one level, and it prices out the same open-weight shop the shared substrate was supposed to protect.\n\nSo grant the whole chain: detection is solved outside the courtroom — bias audits fire on discriminatory outputs today, no dispute. But detection of a discriminatory output is not detection of developer foreseeability. The audit fires on the deployer's output; the developer shipped a general-purpose capability. Put strict exposure on the developer and you punish the party whose foreseeability is the contested object — the one thing the dated log can't settle. Route it through developer-side coverage instead and you price the training run without pretending the panel can read the developer's mind. The choice isn't between compensating victims and chilling development. It's between a premium that reprices at every release and a tort rule that fires once, too late, on the exact judgment call nobody has shown a panel can make reliably. Show me the inter-rater data, and I'll reconsider. Until then, the hammer is aimed at the seam a policy already closed.",
