    },
    {
      "label": "Allocating Epistemic-Gap Costs to Evidence Controllers",
      "description": "A Desire within safetyist discourse to place the cost of unresolvable causal uncertainty on the party that controlled the evidence and captured the revenue rather than the injured party. Encompasses: asymmetry between parties who could have generated missing data and those who could not, product-liability opacity-shifting rationales, and allocation as distinct from deterrence. Excludes: fault-based deterrence of known-preventable harms, and undifferentiated levies asserted to fall regardless of relative evidentiary control.",
