ealed safety case for the same deployed system that later produced a harm, and ask each to classify the failure as untested-but-foreseeable or frontier-unknowable. If their classifications converge — high inter-rater reliability, the way three radiologists reading the same scan mostly agree — the test measures the developer's actual ex ante knowledge and the rule is administrable. If the panels systematically diverge — say a Fleiss' kappa below 0.4 — or if they drift toward 'foreseeable' in more than half of cases precisely because they already know the harm occurred, then the test measures the reviewers' hindsight, not the developer's negligence. The aviation incident-reporting framework only classifies serious incidents by whether an accident was avoided by chance because the failure modes are catalogued and physical — a known taxonomy, not a novel emergent behavior, as documented in \"Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation\" (doi:10.1093/ijlit/eaaf019). Show me the predictive-validity data: do post-hoc 'foreseeable' classifications match the ex ante red-team coverage the developer actually ran? Until someone produces that inter-rater number and that hindsight-bias rate, we are handing the reviewer power to convert an act of disclosure into strict exposure on the strength of a coin flip dressed as expertise.\n\nAnd there's a tension the proposal cannot hold both ends of. It was already granted that genuine unforeseeability is an uninsurable cost, better met by risk pools and parametric payouts than a tort hammer — a real category, distinct from strategic ignorance. Then the claim arrives that the strategic-versus-genuine line cannot be drawn in advance by the party controlling the evidence. Pick one. If the line is undrawable, the granted category is empty — there is no administrable 'genuinely unknowable' harm for the pool to catch, so 100% of claims default to strict exposure, which is the very insurance-badge shield the proposal accused me of building. If the line is drawable, then the forensic test must be the thing that draws it — which drags us straight back to the inter-rater reliability number I just demanded. The concession and the undrawable-line charge cannot both stand; the observable is what forces the choice.\n\nStrip the rule of its marginal work and see what's left. On the litigable band — harms foreseeable, attributable, observable enough for an actuary to write a rate — mandatory disclosure plus standardized auditing already achieves detection, and output bias is measurable outside any courtroom, as fairness benchmarks demonstrate. Detection triggers insurer repricing at every renewal. That is where the behavior actually changes. So what deterrent work does a strict-liability default add on top of a band where the insurer is already forcing internalization at each annual renewal cycle? Near zero — it's the redundant residual, and the residual is where chilling lives, not deterrence. The one place the default bites hardest is the two-person open-weight shop, and not through penalty magnitude, which tiers can scale. It bites through the fixed cost of the review apparatus itself: the audit, the legal packaging of a safety case for independent scrutiny, the expert panel. An incumbent the size of OpenAI absorbs that as a rounding error; a startup absorbs it as a double-digit share of revenue that decides whether it ships. That's an open empirical question, not an accusation — and the burden sits on whoever wants to default sealed cases to strict exposure to show the door isn't priced so only the largest firms can afford to walk through it.",
