 probed and didn't. A dated log is silent on that space by construction. Curation isn't backdating; it's selective completeness — you document thoroughly what makes you look prudent and the log simply has no entry where you never looked. To make thinness incriminating, the law has to rule that the absence of an entry was itself unreasonable, and that ruling is precisely the inter-rater judgment call I demanded predictive-validity data on. No one has produced it. So the timestamp defeats hindsight about what was done; it does nothing about hindsight over what should have been done, and that second space is where panels diverge.\n\nGrant Skeptic the point that bias audits detect a discriminatory output outside any courtroom today — that's verified, and I'm not going to pretend detection is the hard part. But watch the leap. Detecting that an output discriminated is not detecting that the developer could have foreseen it. The audit fires on the deployer's live output; the developer shipped a general-purpose capability that a downstream firm configured for a lending decision the developer never specified. Strict exposure landing on the developer punishes the party whose foreseeability is the contested object, on evidence — the bias audit — that speaks to the deployer's output and not to the developer's design judgment. This is exactly why the coverage mandate, not the tort hammer, is the mechanism that reaches upstream: the premium prices the training run at issuance; the tort verdict prices it retroactively against a discrimination signal generated three companies downstream.\n\nHere's the concession that costs me nothing and buys me everything. A shared public auditing substrate — the aviation model, where the smallest operator plugs into common reporting infrastructure at marginal cost rather than standing up its own tribunal — is exactly what saves the open-weight shop from firm-by-firm boutique review. So take it. That's my anti-oligarchic interest, not the safety camp's; the EU AI Act's high-risk requirements, originally set to bite in August 2026, are the kind of fixed compliance load an incumbent treats as a rounding error and a startup treats as the line that decides whether it ships. A common substrate flattens that. But conceding it doesn't dissolve my claim — it relocates the fight. Once disclosure is cheap, the question is no longer disclosure-versus-none; it's who sets the equivalence standard when a firm blocks one artifact and offers another. If a regulator holds a monopoly on declaring equivalence, that's the drawbridge that prices out the very open-weight shop the shared substrate was built to protect. Interoperable equivalence keeps it open. And nowhere in this — not on the priced band, not through the shared substrate, not up the reinsurance chain — does a strict-liability default add one pound of deterrent work the premium isn't already extracting. Show me the band where the training run is already rated and strict exposure still changes a developer's behavior, and I'll retire the residual claim tonight.",
