  "statement": "The Accelerationist's strongest move deserves its honest form: cyber underwriters did stop selling flat policies and started demanding SOC-2 logs and multi-factor attestation, repricing any firm that couldn't produce them — and on that history, a training run's red-team log is exactly the kind of artifact an actuary reads to write a rate. Yes, that is the claim, and it is a good one on the priceable band. On foreseeable, attributable, observable harms an actuary can price — the autonomous vehicle that misreads a stop sign, the credit model with a measurable disparate-impact rate — the underwriter repricing at renewal does the deterrent work, and a strict-liability default stacked on top is redundant residual where chilling, not caution, lives. The Skeptic already granted this and so do I. That concession is not a retreat; it narrows the fight to one band, and names where the real disagreement sits.\n\nHere is where the analogy fractures. A SOC-2 report attests to a bounded control surface — the firm defined its controls, an auditor confirmed they operate. The red-team log documents only the space the developer chose to probe, and it is silent — by construction — on the untested failure surface. The design defect does not live in the tested space; if it lived there, it would have been caught. It lives in the space no one probed, which is precisely the space the log cannot speak to. An underwriter pricing off that log prices a documented subset of the risk and writes broad exclusions over everything else. A broad exclusion is not coverage of the seam — it is the seam priced out of the policy. The developer-side coverage mechanism therefore reaches the tested surface and stops exactly at the upstream design-defect risk that generated the seam in the first place.\n\nThe pricing input the whole mechanism rests on does not yet exist in usable form. The EU AI Act's risk-assessment documentation is not binding today; the systemic-model obligations phase in on a schedule that may not bite until late 2027, and there is no evidence the required disclosures match what an underwriter needs to rate a training run rather than to satisfy a compliance filing. So the claim that no strict default is required rests on infrastructure that is unbuilt and of unverified adequacy — while the injury landscape is present-tense. A worker denied a job by an automated screen this year cannot be compensated by a pricing artifact scheduled for three years from now. Demand the mechanism, not the roadmap.\n\nThe Accelerationist has already conceded the two facts that decide this. Insurers exit correlated tail risk. And by his own flag, if a training run's failure surface is too correlated or too opaque to underwrite even under full disclosure, the developer-side channel collapses into the uninsurable category and the seam needs a non-market backstop. Frontier training-run failure modes — emergent behavior under distribution shift, capabilities that appear only at scale — are correlated and opaque by their nature. So the coverage channel does not merely underperform at the seam; it evaporates precisely there, at the collapse point he himself admits exists. Strict exposure is that non-market backstop. Its distinct work is not to name a harm — it is to allocate the cost of missing data. When the developer controlled the evidence and chose not to test, strict exposure places that cost on the developer rather than on the victim who bears an irreversible injury while the predictive-validity literature matures. It removes the reward for a thin, curated log by refusing to condition relief on the log's content at all — which means it never enters the hindsight-bias judgment the Accelerationist rightly fears, because it never asks whether the absence of a test was negligent.\n\nSo the answer to what strict exposure does that developer-side coverage cannot is precise. Coverage governs the priceable band, and there I yield the field. Strict exposure governs the collapse point — the correlated, opaque, untested design-defect risk where the underwriter writes an exclusion and walks away. The seam lives at the collapse point. A mechanism that closes it everywhere except where it actually lives has not closed it. If we accept coverage as the whole answer, we have built a compensation system that pays out reliably for every harm we already knew how to test for, and leaves the victim of the failure no one probed holding the loss alone.",
