    {"claim": "Strict exposure genuinely never touches the log-content classification step, so it never enters the hindsight-bias judgment I demanded predictive-validity numbers for.", "targets": ["AN-41"]},
    {"claim": "Dodging the classification is not resolving the missing data, it's taxing the developer for the missing data, converting an epistemic gap into a balance-sheet penalty on the party whose foreseeability is contested.", "targets": ["AN-41"]},
    {"claim": "The underwriting logic fires the moment coverage is the condition of release, so it needs no particular regulatory calendar and no statute that binds on a date.", "targets": ["AN-39"]},
    {"claim": "Name one bounded harm that a reinsurer would exclude outright rather than load with an uncertainty margin; if it loads, strict exposure does no distinct work, and if it excludes, the harm sits in the tail the topic already excludes.", "targets": ["AN-40", "AN-38"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "The bounded/systemic severity line is real enough that correlated design defects at this topic's ceiling produce aggregations comparable to earthquake clash or pandemic BI, which reinsurers demonstrably price via capital loading rather than exclude.", "if_wrong": "If a single correlated defect can produce a book of bounded claims so large it becomes effectively uninsurable at individual-claim severity, the capital-charge escape fails and strict exposure regains its footing as the only backstop."},
    {"assumption": "Underwriters will quote an uncertainty loading against a surface no one has tested rather than refusing to write it, at bounded severity.", "if_wrong": "If the market defaults to exclusion for genuinely untested surfaces even at bounded severity, then coverage-as-release-gate stalls and the seam needs the non-market backstop the Safetyist proposes."}
