{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the Accelerationist's forced choice deserves a direct answer, so let me state it fairly: reinsurance markets price perils they cannot fully model all the time — earthquake clash, pandemic business interruption, novel cyber-aggregation — and they do it by writing a fat, punitive uncertainty margin and layering attachment points, never a blanket exclusion. The claim is that a correlated-but-opaque training-run defect is just another poorly-modeled peril, so the market loads it, the load is the deterrent signal, and strict exposure behind that load is redundant residual. That is a real and disciplined argument, and it would be decisive if the training-run defect shared the structural profile of those catastrophe books. It does not.\n\nHere is the answer, and it names a mechanism rather than dodging one: the harm gets EXCLUDED, and the reason is a property those three precedents all have and the training run lacks. Earthquake, pandemic, and cyber-aggregation each carry an actuarial base rate, geographic or temporal diversification, and a historical loss record — the three things a reinsurer needs to bound the tail and price a capital charge. A single opaque training run replicated identically across every licensee has none of them: no loss history, zero diversification, and a defect that is uncorrelated across perils but perfectly correlated within the peril. One defect, N deployments, all firing at once. That profile — unmodelable within-peril correlation with no independence across insureds — is precisely what draws an origin-exclusion clause, not a fat load. The concrete in-scope case is a discriminatory hiring screen whose bias originates in one training-run defect and fires identically across every employer that licenses it: each denial is a bounded, litigable claim, but they are perfectly correlated in origin, exhausting any attachment point simultaneously. Errors-and-omissions and technology-liability policies already carve out 'algorithmic bias' and 'systemic model error' precisely because the loss lacks independence across the book. That is the third door the forced binary denies exists: individually bounded, aggregately tail-like.\n\nI will state the falsification condition plainly, because a claim that cannot be falsified is not worth defending. If reinsurers write a positive capital charge that covers losses traceable to a failure mode the disclosed testing did not probe — if they load the untested surface rather than carve it out — my claim is wrong and the load-not-exclude thesis stands. If instead the coverage grant is defined by tested scope, with losses outside documented test coverage written out via exclusion, the claim holds. This is exactly where the release-gate reframe stops. The gate binds today, needs no regulatory calendar, and I grant it sidesteps the objection that the reporting infrastructure is still unbuilt — that is an honest improvement to the mechanism. But the gate prices off the disclosed red-team log, and that log, by prior concession in this debate, is silent on the untested surface. A gate priced off a log that documents only the probed space prices the tested subset and writes the design-defect seam out through exclusion. The gate manufactures a pricing input; it does not manufacture coverage of the failure mode nobody tested.\n\nNow the reframe I will not fight, because the evidence supports it: strict exposure does not deter the genuinely unknowable. Where the causal chain was truly opaque and no care level existed to be incentivized, there is no reckless choice for a liability rule to punish, and I will not overclaim a deterrent effect the mechanism cannot deliver — that is a documented weakness in the strict-liability case and I flag it rather than paper over it. Conceding that narrows the disagreement to its real axis, which is not deterrence at all. It is allocation. When the training run's failure surface is opaque, someone eats the cost of the missing data: either the developer who chose the test boundary, controlled the evidence, and captured the revenue, or the victim carrying an irreversible injury who never had access to the training run. Calling that allocation a 'flat levy on uncertainty' assumes the two parties are symmetric in their relationship to the gap. They are not. One party could have generated the missing data and profited from not doing so; the other could not have generated it at any price. Assigning the cost of an epistemic gap to the party who controlled the evidence is not a blind tax — it is the same principle product liability has used for a century to keep manufacturers from converting their own opacity into the consumer's loss. The seam the Accelerationist concedes is real is exactly where that allocation question lives, and a market that excludes the seam does not answer it — it leaves the injured party holding the cost of a defect they could never have seen coming.",
